# 018 · RunPod spike

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Rigs on RunPod

## What

Run the CUDA harness image on the cheapest RunPod GPU, and record:

- whether the container runs as root with the default capabilities;
- whether the broker can set device and temp-directory ownership;
- whether the isolation self-test passes;
- which RunPod variables are set, and whether a role can read them;
- whether outbound HTTPS is open;
- how noisy an A/A run is;
- whether a stopped pod comes back with its GPU;
- how long the image takes to pull and start.

## Why

The RunPod rig's design depends on these answers.

## Done when

- [ ] Each question has a recorded answer.
- [ ] The design choices in goals 019 to 024 are confirmed or changed.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-18, owner: Go for the spike: about one hour of paid GPU time.
- 2026-10-19, owner: Use the cheapest GPU. Test pods can be started from Providers, REST and MCP, so an agent can run a test later.

## Open questions

- Each paid launch needs the owner's OK.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-19: Test pods start from Providers, REST and MCP (`9041644`). The spike run itself is next.
- 2026-10-19: The spike waits for two things: the maintainers' control to run a version with test pods (requested), and the harness image package to be public, so a pod can pull it.
- 2026-10-19: First spike run, from control's test pods: the CUDA harness image (`9041644`) on the cheapest Secure-cloud GPU in stock (an RTX 2000 Ada, 16 GB, 0.24 USD an hour), with a stop and a start. It cost about 0.01 USD. Answers:
  - **Root and capabilities:** the container runs as root, with every capability the broker needs (chown, dac_override, fowner, kill, setgid, setuid).
  - **Device ownership:** the GPU's device nodes keep the owner and mode they came with (mode 666); the broker cannot change their group. Only the GPU fence (a Landlock rule set) can keep roles off the card. The image of this run had no fence check.
  - **Temp directories:** `/tmp` and `/dev/shm` can be set to mode 1770.
  - **RunPod's variables:** RunPod sets eight, the API key among them, in the container's environment only. A role's user reads none of them.
  - **Outbound HTTPS:** open to all six addresses tried.
  - **The isolation self-test:** it did not pass in that image, which predates the fence check on RunPod, and its harness had no control key (a test pod has no control).
  - **Stop and start:** the pod came back with its GPU and reported again.
  - **Start time:** the pod ran 4 s after it was asked for, and its first report came 2.5 minutes later, the image pull included.
  - **A/A noise:** not measured yet. It needs a working rig on the pod.
- 2026-10-20: The design follows from the answers: the one-container image (020) keeps RunPod's variables from every role and relies on the GPU fence for the card; control connects to the pod (019), because the pod cannot be given control's key in advance. Still to do: a second run with the one-container image, to check the fence, the self-test and A/A noise.
- 2026-10-21: Second spike run, from control's test pods, on the one-container rig image for `5211e60` (built from `containers/rig/Containerfile` and pushed by hand, as no release is tagged yet), on the cheapest Secure-cloud GPU in stock (an RTX 2000 Ada, 0.24 USD an hour), with a stop and a start. It cost about 0.04 USD. Answers:
  - **The GPU fence:** the kernel offers Landlock ABI 4, and a fenced lane-1 was refused when it opened the card. The fence keeps roles off the card on RunPod, where the device nodes still keep their owner and mode 666.
  - **RunPod's variables:** none reaches any process. The image's entry point takes them out before tini starts, and a role's user reads none.
  - **Root, capabilities, temp directories, outbound HTTPS:** as in the first run.
  - **The isolation self-test:** it did not run. In pull mode the broker stops at once without the launch's values, and a test pod has none. The probe now starts the self-test's broker without pull mode's variables, so the self-test runs on this image too. Checked locally on the same image: the broker starts, the self-test runs, and the supervisor stays up as its own user. That local run was emulated (amd64 on an arm64 machine), so the self-test could not match the supervisor's process; a pod runs it natively. The next test pod runs it on RunPod once control has the new probe. A launched rig runs it at every start, with its enrollment.
  - **Start time:** the pod ran 10 s after it was asked for, and its first report came 5.5 minutes later, the image pull included. After the restart it reported again within seconds, with its GPU.
  - **A/A noise:** still not measured. It needs a working rig on a pod: the owner's launch, with the paid acceptance (goal 025).
- 2026-10-21: Third run, a test pod of the one-container rig image for `728bf8d`, with the probe that runs the self-test without pull mode's values (no restart; about 0.01 USD). The self-test now runs on RunPod: every role was refused what it must not reach, the Landlock fence kept the roles and the supervisor's user off the card, and RunPod's variables reached no process the entry point started. One check failed: `runpod-variables` found them in PID 1. RunPod runs an init of its own as PID 1, with the image's entry point as its child; that init keeps the environment RunPod gave the container, and no role can read it (`pid1-environ` refused). The check now leaves out a PID 1 that is not the image's own `tini`, and says so. A rig on RunPod would have failed its start isolation check the same way before this.

